      "id": "article-mergers-acquisitions-value-creation",
      "type": "article",
      "lang": "en",
      "status": "published",
      "category": "Strategy",
      "title": "Mergers & Acquisitions: Create Value After the Deal",
      "slug": "mergers-acquisitions-value-creation",
      "excerpt": "Mergers and acquisitions are not simply transactions between two companies In many cases an acquisition can be the fastest way to enter a new market add capabilities the business does not currently have or gain",
      "content": "Mergers and acquisitions are not simply transactions between two companies\n\nIn many cases an acquisition can be the fastest way to enter a new market add capabilities the business does not currently have or gain access to a larger customer base\n\nBut a good deal does not start with the price\n\nIt starts with understanding why the acquisition makes sense\n\nWhat does the target company bring to the business\n\nCan it open a new market\n\nDoes it have a product technology or distribution network the company needs\n\nCan the combined business operate more efficiently\n\nAnd most importantly can the two companies actually work together after the deal is completed\n\nMany acquisitions look attractive on paper but struggle after closing because too much attention was given to completing the transaction and not enough to what happens afterwards\n\nPeople culture systems customers contracts and daily operations all become part of the equation\n\nThat is why the success of a merger or acquisition should not be measured by completing the deal\n\nIt should be measured by the value the business is able to create after the deal\n\nA good acquisition should make the company stronger not simply bigger\n\nBusiness Owner",
